  abstract = {The ionization potential of exo-tetrahydrodicyclopentadiene (exo-JP-10, C10H16), a strained hydrocarbon central to advanced propulsion, has remained unresolved for decades due to its cage-like structure, the inherent instability of its cation, and the absence of reliable theoretical predictions. The first ionization potential (IP) was reported once at 9.35 \textpm{} 0.05 eV in 1973 yet it has resisted consistent theoretical validation. In this study, we present the first quantitative confirmation using density functional theory (DFT) based {$\Delta$}SCF, Hartree--Fock (HF), and outer valence Green's function (OVGF) methods. The {$\Delta$}SCF(DFT) and G4 composite methods well reproduces the experimental IP at 9.31\,eV and 9.27 eV, respectively, whereas Koopmans' Theorem (HF) and standard OVGF systematically overestimate the value. The second-order OVGF method (OVGF(2)) not only yields a first IP for JP-10 that is consistent with the {$\Delta$}SCF result, but also provides outer-valence IPs extending up to 17.5\,eV. Orbital analysis reveals that ionization perturbs {$\alpha$} electrons more strongly than {$\beta$} in the valence space (a {$\beta$} electron is removed at ionization), as well as substantial shifts in the carbon 1s core electron energies. These electronic reorganizations are mirrored in the calculated 13C NMR spectrum, where the junction carbons (C1-C4) and C10, C6/C7 undergo pronounced deshielding. This study delivers the first rigorously validated theoretical value for the ionization potential of JP-10, closing a 50-year gap in understanding this high-energy-density hydrocarbon. By establishing {$\Delta$}SCF as a reliable approach for characterizing unstable cations and leveraging OVGF(2) to compute outer-valence IPs up to 17.5\,eV, the present study links core- and valence-level electronic reorganizations to measurable spectroscopic signatures. These insights not only 2 advance JP-10 ionization process but also deepen our understanding of strained hydrocarbon architectures, providing a foundation for the rational design of thermally stable, electronically tunable molecules for sustainable aviation fuel (SAF) development and advanced propulsion systems.},
